in seven minutes or less. I want to share with you some thoughts

97
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:47,920
about peace scouting and the kind of opportunities that we have

98
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,280
as scouters to promote peace.

99
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:54,480
And this is based on an address that Baden Powell delivered to what

100
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:57,880
would become the World Organization of the Scouting Movement in 1922

101
00:05:57,880 --> 00:05:58,840
in Paris.

102
00:05:58,840 --> 00:06:01,360
And then I have a couple of email questions to answer.

103
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:04,240
So let's get started. shall we

104
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:08,360
Scout MasterChip in seven minutes or less?

105
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:18,719
Since the podcast last week, the world

106
00:06:18,719 --> 00:06:21,960
is reeling from the latest episode of terrorism that

107
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:23,880
unfolded in Paris, France,

108
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:27,680
And that all too familiar specter of fear and foreboding

109
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:30,080
is blanketing all of our news coverage.

110
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:34,120
But if you're a scouter, you get to respond to what

111
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:36,840
happened in Paris this week when you

112
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:40,240
go to open the door of the room where your scouts are

113
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:43,880
going to meet, because you have the opportunity

114
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:49,080
to have a profound effect on the collective future

115
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:50,400
of humanity.

116
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:51,840
Sounds like a big order, doesn't it?

117
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:53,760
But it's actually pretty simple.

118
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:56,720
The idea I want to share with you isn't new

119
00:06:56,760 --> 00:06:58,520
And it isn't especially complicated,

120
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:01,040
And because it's neither new or complicated,

121
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:02,960
it's going to sound a little trite,

122
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:04,200
But don't be fooled.

123
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:07,840
This is a profound and inspiring opportunity

124
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:11,000
for every scouter in every part of the world

125
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,920
Scouting's founder, Robert Baden Powell,

126
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:17,600
shared this very simple idea in an address to what

127
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:19,880
would become the World Organization of the Scouting

128
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:24,000
Movement in 1922 at the Sorbonne University there

129
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,400
in Paris,

130
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:27,880
In the very city that has been just reeling

131
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:31,840
from these acts of terrorism. many years ago, there

132
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:35,120
was Baden Powell at the world famous Sorbonne

133
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:41,400
University delivering an address about education in love

134
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:43,120
in place of fear.

135
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:48,360
Imagine for a moment 1922 in world history.

136
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:51,520
Humanity is still reeling from the tragedy of World War

137
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:56,400
I. Baden Powell himself, his own personal history,

138
00:07:56,400 --> 00:07:59,799
tells us that he has an intimate personal knowledge

139
00:07:59,799 --> 00:08:03,799
of war during his long service as a British officer

140
00:08:03,799 --> 00:08:04,960
in the Boer Wars.

141
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:07,919
And in his address he says, quote:

142
00:08:07,919 --> 00:08:11,479
I've spent a good part of my life as a soldier

143
00:08:11,479 --> 00:08:14,240
And I've seen something of the brutality and horrors

144
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:17,760
incidental to this man-authorized murder

145
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:20,760
of God's creatures, our brother men,

146
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:24,159
together with the ruin of their homes and the sufferings

147
00:08:24,159 --> 00:08:26,360
of the innocent women and children.

148
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:28,840
And it is in this historic backdrop,

149
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:33,520
not only of what's happening in world affairs in 1922,

150
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:36,360
but Baden Powell's own personal history,

151
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:38,799
that he steps onto the stage at the Sorbonne

152
00:08:38,799 --> 00:08:42,679
University in Paris and shares these thoughts.

153
00:08:42,679 --> 00:08:45,720
He begins by stating that fear is what

154
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:49,080
compels mankind and nations to harm each other.

155
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:51,320
And that fear quote-

156
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:53,680
has been the weapon of the powerful

157
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:56,360
in terrorizing the weaker. unquote.

158
00:08:56,360 --> 00:08:58,440
He follows by observing that quote:

159
00:08:58,440 --> 00:09:02,640
love is what we all pray for, yet we allow ourselves

160
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:05,560
to live under the yoke of fear.

161
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:10,080
And he insists that if we imply love in place of fear,

162
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:15,080
we gain peace, mutual trust, honor and goodwill.

163
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:19,360
And it goes on to note something very counterintuitive:

164
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:23,960
The world's just been through World War I,

165
00:09:23,960 --> 00:09:27,720
but there's rising militarism amongst the nations of Europe

166
00:09:27,720 --> 00:09:30,760
in the wake of that war based on fear.

167
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:33,360
But Baden Powell, in his address, suggests

168
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:36,880
that there is an alternative for militarism.

169
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:39,720
He says there's a way for every nation

170
00:09:39,720 --> 00:09:42,280
to have strong, character-driven young people,

171
00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:46,079
confident in themselves, resilient, educated,

172
00:09:46,079 --> 00:09:49,280
compassionate people who will fix the world.

173
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:51,880
He continues by saying- quote:

174
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:55,880
this may seem to be a utopian dream,

175
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:59,040
too visionary and too impractical,

176
00:09:59,040 --> 00:10:03,839
And he asks: can it ever be brought about in practice?

177
00:10:03,839 --> 00:10:05,640
And then he answers his own question: yes,

178
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:09,800
because through scouting it's already happening

179
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:11,800
in every corner of the world.

180
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:13,400
And that's where you and I come in.

181
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:18,200
We can apply love in the place of fear. sometime this week

182
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:22,280
We can take part in creating strong,

183
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:25,800
character-driven young people, confident in themselves,

184
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:29,760
resilient, educated and compassionate people

185
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:31,240
who will fix the world.

186
00:10:31,240 --> 00:10:34,240
When we turn the key on the meeting room door

187
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:35,560
and our scouts fill the room,

188
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:39,200
we're unlocking an opportunity to apply love

189
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:40,480
in the place of fear.

190
00:10:40,480 --> 00:10:43,280
And our dens and patrols and crews,

191
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:47,160
scouts, work together and learn that they can get along,

192
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:49,560
that they can resolve their differences peacefully

193
00:10:49,560 --> 00:10:53,040
and they can be of service to the greater circles

194
00:10:53,040 --> 00:10:55,680
of their family, their community,

195
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:58,000
their nation and their world.

196
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,120
In the course of the last week

197
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:00,040
there have been some events

198
00:11:00,040 --> 00:11:02,200
that make us feel uncertain and fearful,

199
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:04,360
But you know, it's kind of hard to find a time

200
00:11:04,360 --> 00:11:06,960
in human history where there wasn't a good excuse

201
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:09,680
to feel uncertain and fearful every single day.

202
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:12,920
But the simple truth is that as scouts,

203
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:15,680
we build our world in comparatively small

204
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:17,200
but incredibly important ways

205
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:19,600
by forming character, compassion

206
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:23,040
and the ideal of service in our scouts.

207
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:26,320
Your opportunity as a scouter is very profound.

208
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:28,720
Take a moment today and think about that,

209
00:11:28,720 --> 00:11:31,840
because we join hands with millions

210
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:33,640
of fellow scouts around the world

211
00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:37,880
working to educate in love in place of fear.

212
00:11:37,880 --> 00:11:39,000
As I said at the beginning,

213
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:40,720
it's a simple idea,

214
00:11:40,720 --> 00:11:45,600
It's nothing new, but it's so very, very important

215
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:48,040
because we all have plenty of work left to do.

